1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:10,552
This is the adversary. It audits how a result was produced, the way a hard reviewer would, and it re-runs the code rather than taking the write-up's word.

2
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:21,291
Its file says what it looks for. Data leakage between training and test. Claims that seem too good. Whether a computation is correct and reproducible.

3
00:00:21,291 --> 00:00:31,004
And when the work carries intellectual property, it refers that to the lawyer itself, and folds the lawyer's verdict into its own.

4
00:00:31,399 --> 00:00:43,181
It audits; it does not build. It does not produce figures. It never presents speculation as fact: what it has not checked by running code is labelled as speculated.

5
00:00:43,181 --> 00:00:51,547
And it never launders a disagreement into a verdict. Ambiguous evidence is reported as ambiguous.

6
00:00:51,949 --> 00:00:59,551
A real run, on the synthetic example that ships with Murmurent. The member asks whether a claimed result is sound.

7
00:00:59,551 --> 00:01:16,434
The verdict is Questions. The number reproduces exactly, but the adversary found that the two groups come from different countries, and that the Methods text describes a step the code does not take. The last line is the mandatory counsel note.

8
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:27,331
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the adversary to audit an experiment or a write-up. Give it the folder with the code and the data, not a summary.

9
00:01:27,331 --> 00:01:44,445
The first line is the verdict: Pass, Questions or Reject. Every finding says whether it was observed or speculated, and the audit ends with the counsel line. Run it before someone else does.
